i thought instead of giving you an  overview which is probably uh you can  get from really the website of the  commission which is  hiv law  commission.org i try and make the link  between the topics uh to be discussed in  the next couple of days and the work of  the commission  and that's criminalization and  what's the commission doing in in  relation to that you'll get a lot of  information on the commission  like i said on the website  um it's it's it's it's it's real goal is  to develop actionable evidence informed  rights-based recommendations for  effective aids responses that mitigate  the impact of hiv and promote and  protect the human rights of people  living with and most vulnerable to hiv i  i mean this is a very dry boring jargon  filled statement but i think the idea is  that the commission  um  makes  these  recommendations not to lie on the  shelves but to be used by policymakers  and to be used by advocates to actually  see that actionable stuff happens on the  ground and that reform takes place  um and i could talk a lot about all  about how things already moving in  somewhat in certain directions but but  we don't have the time happy to take  questions  uh  during breaks and stuff um  so  so here so some of the objectives the  commission were to analyze existing  evidence and generate new evidence and  i'll explain to you how some of that has  happened because it relates directly to  this topic  um  develop the recommendations increase  awareness amongst key constituencies and  so there's been a whole method by which  we've tried to engage law and policy  makers at national levels with um and  civil society bring them together have a  conversation increase awareness  revive some of the conversation which  has kind of fallen by the wayside around  a lot of issues on hiv and the law  and actually encourage a public dialogue  and i just got back actually  interestingly enough two weeks ago from  a very public dialogue  on hiv and the law in belize  where the entire dialogue was broadcast  on a radio channel which is listened to  by everyone in the country to the whole  country so everyone was listening in and  the government of course got completely  stressed out and i was harassed at  immigration  but uh it was it was interesting that we  just opened up the whole conversation  around sexuality and morality and stuff  and it was a  so we're already seeing a little bit of  uh movement  um and then like i said engage with  civil society empower civil society to  actually be able to advocate for change  uh the commissioners you will get their  details again on the website  this is the these are the four focus  areas of the community  it must kind of look at laws and  practices that effectively criminalize  and discriminate against people so  that's very broad language but it  certainly includes criminalization of  transmission exposure as it does  criminalization of sexual orientation or  sodomy uh criminalization of sex work  laws and practices in mitigator  sustained violence and discrimination is  lived by women which includes  gaps in the law of vis-a-vis sexual  assault laws domestic violence laws etc  and how they impact our vulnerability  and of course women who are hiv-positive  and how discrimination reinforces their  marginalization and actually affects  their health  uh treatment access issues related to  the law particularly patent law and then  finally issues of loan hiv pertaining to  children that's a variety of things  including age of consent issues  the commission will uh which began its  work in june 2010  it will conclude a by early next year so  we have a time frame and all this has  happened pretty rapidly  uh but it's happened with a lot of  consultations so i think there's a lot  of mobilization around  uh this just taken place in the last 18  months  um  i thought this would uh i i thought i  should share with you this which is most  kind of  uh topical for this this this  this meeting we're having now what are  the outputs of vis-a-vis criminalization  of hiv transmission exposure that will  come out of this process well there are  two working papers that have been  written  uh the working papers are going to be  published but they're presently being  finalized they'll be on the website and  hopefully published in a journal of some  sort uh there is matthew wheat's paper  which is a global review of the law on  criminalization of hiv exposure and  transmission and then scott barris and  matthew have written a paper on the  criminalization morality  uh  moral responsibility for sexual  transmission  and then we have this whole process of  regional dialogues uh so we've had seven  of these across the world where we've  got civil society and government  to sit across the table and have a  conversation  on a lot of these issues and this  process  called for submissions to be made by  civil society on matters that affect  them academics  people in the field people affected  uh  legal aid providers human rights  activists etc so we received about 650  submissions across the globe 62 of which  related to criminalization of hiv  transmission exposure that's sort of the  breakup of where most a lot of them came  from  and an analysis of submissions is  underway right now actually uh which  will also be published but what i share  with you right now is a very very early  stage of that analysis  we received so out of those 62 you could  see  submissions discussed the the blue  lines are specific laws criminalizing  hiv and then the red ones talk about the  application of general criminal law to  hiv and so you get a sense of  where  people uh activists etc are feeling  possibly impact and a lot of this is of  course anecdotal it's it's very much  about an individual experience it's not  necessarily scientific  you know research  but it's about often legal aid provision  in these contexts etc  and so i thought i'd just give you a  very quick overview of  well  how  what what what these submissions are  telling us  uh in three categories or well the first  is on hiv specific laws uh and we found  mostly these were from africa and high  income countries  either an omnibus hiv legislation or in  criminal code specifically introduced on  hiv  sometimes criminalizes non-disclosure to  spouses  a lot of the concerns that were  expressed include issues of the fact  that intent is not required and a lot of  these laws significant risk is not  required  uh risk reduction and disclosure or  consent are not considered as grounds  for defense there are disproportionate  sentences vague language the failure of  the law to account for the changing  implications of an hiv diagnosis in  terms of treatment  and then  matched with a lack of real proper legal  advice to defend oneself  and a weak evidence accepted by the  court so the rigour of evidence is not  really a standard that the courts  necessarily follow and you know some of  the submissions even talked about how  prejudice is reflected in the court in  the judiciary itself on a lot of these  issues  and uh impact in africa for instance  even though there were no prosecutions  there was a sense that existence of  these laws are  uh are known to people and could  potentially deter people from testing  and there's a disproportionate impact on  women in particular culture in cultural  and traditional contexts uh impact and  stigma the mere presence the law of  fuels hiv related stigma within society  and reflected in the court cases and  court pronouncements  uh and there's association with police  successes um an unlawful arrest physical  violence refusal to grant lawful  entitlements to hiv positive people in  prisons  then there were the then there were some  submissions related to  criminalization in general criminal  codes  again mostly in high-income countries  the application offenses relating to  transmission  of infectious diseases generally those  are the general criminal codes within  which this was captured uh and then  there's the application of general  criminal offences like aggravated  assault reckless endangerment etc um  there's an inconsistent jurisprudence  that has been that has been generated  through this and there's certainly a  skewed racial impact that was felt  um and then there's the third type  type of law under which a lot of this  gets captured which is public health law  fewer submissions  such laws visit the white powers in the  state  for example to isolate people living  with hiv regardless of conduct  and uh often such laws effectively  criminalize uh because migrant workers  for for instance are forcibly tested and  incarcerated and deported as a  consequence of their hiv status  um and finally certainly some positive  examples uh there have been challenges  to laws that criminalize hiv exposure  and transmission through advocacy in the  courts or with legislators so we've seen  that for instance in europe  um  and then there are there's success  because of arguments of obsolescence and  on on enforceability  vagueness and arbitrariness of these  laws which have convinced lawmakers to  take a new uh kind of take a new  position on some of the stuff and then  prosecutorial guidelines which exist in  england and wales  so that gives you an overview of how the  commission is actually  going to be engaged in some of these  issues

